1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,831
A bus pulls away.

2
00:00:01,831 --> 00:00:03,530
A lift starts to rise.

3
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:05,375
A swing changes direction.

4
00:00:05,775 --> 00:00:07,417
You feel a push each time.

5
00:00:07,417 --> 00:00:10,874
But at a steady speed, even
a fast one, you feel nothing.

6
00:00:11,324 --> 00:00:14,511
Aarav keeps a water bottle
in his bicycle basket.

7
00:00:14,861 --> 00:00:17,639
When he speeds up or
brakes, the water tilts.

8
00:00:17,639 --> 00:00:20,779
When he cruises, it stays
flat, even at top speed.

9
00:00:21,279 --> 00:00:23,508
The water does not feel the speed.

10
00:00:23,508 --> 00:00:25,098
It feels something else.

11
00:00:25,098 --> 00:00:27,045
Today we draw it as a graph.

12
00:00:27,695 --> 00:00:30,068
So what is an acceleration-time graph?

13
00:00:30,418 --> 00:00:32,270
Time goes along the bottom.

14
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:34,269
Acceleration goes up the side.

15
00:00:34,669 --> 00:00:38,687
Each point says how fast the
velocity is changing at that moment.

16
00:00:39,087 --> 00:00:41,108
Position-time tells us where.

17
00:00:41,108 --> 00:00:43,353
Velocity-time tells us how fast.

18
00:00:43,353 --> 00:00:47,458
Acceleration-time tells us how
quickly that speed is changing.

19
00:00:48,108 --> 00:00:52,025
Let's replay Aarav's ride from
last time, with the water bottle.

20
00:00:52,375 --> 00:00:57,237
He speeds up at one point five metres
per second squared, for four seconds.

21
00:00:57,237 --> 00:00:59,875
The graph is a flat line, above zero.

22
00:01:00,275 --> 00:01:03,023
Then he cruises at six metres per second.

23
00:01:03,023 --> 00:01:05,099
The acceleration drops to zero.

24
00:01:05,099 --> 00:01:06,572
The water goes flat.

25
00:01:06,972 --> 00:01:09,997
Then he brakes at minus
two, for three seconds.

26
00:01:09,997 --> 00:01:11,771
The graph jumps below zero.

27
00:01:12,171 --> 00:01:14,038
Slow motion at the switch.

28
00:01:14,038 --> 00:01:18,550
The velocity arrow stays long, but
the acceleration arrow vanishes in an

29
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:19,450
instant.

30
00:01:19,667 --> 00:01:22,744
So uniform acceleration makes a flat line.

31
00:01:22,744 --> 00:01:25,600
A change in acceleration makes a jump.

32
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:28,170
Where do these values come from?

33
00:01:28,170 --> 00:01:30,308
From the velocity-time graph above it.

34
00:01:30,658 --> 00:01:31,558
Step one.

35
00:01:31,544 --> 00:01:36,374
The acceleration at any moment is
the slope of the velocity-time graph.

36
00:01:37,124 --> 00:01:38,024
Step two.

37
00:01:37,973 --> 00:01:40,849
A straight line rising has a fixed slope.

38
00:01:40,849 --> 00:01:44,045
So the acceleration is
a flat line above zero.

39
00:01:44,795 --> 00:01:45,812
Step three.

40
00:01:45,812 --> 00:01:48,541
A flat velocity line has zero slope.

41
00:01:48,541 --> 00:01:51,038
So the acceleration sits on zero.

42
00:01:51,038 --> 00:01:53,756
Falling lines give values below zero.

43
00:01:54,506 --> 00:01:55,684
Side by side.

44
00:01:55,684 --> 00:01:59,940
On a velocity-time graph, a
flat line means steady velocity.

45
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:05,058
On an acceleration-time graph, a flat
line at two means the velocity grows

46
00:02:05,058 --> 00:02:07,860
by two metres per second, every second.

47
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:09,609
Now you try.

48
00:02:09,609 --> 00:02:12,465
An acceleration-time graph sits on zero.

49
00:02:12,465 --> 00:02:14,653
Must the object be at rest?

50
00:02:14,653 --> 00:02:16,081
Pause and decide.

51
00:02:19,281 --> 00:02:20,181
No!

52
00:02:19,768 --> 00:02:23,675
Zero acceleration only means
the velocity does not change.

53
00:02:23,675 --> 00:02:27,931
It could be cruising at six
metres per second, or at sixty.

54
00:02:28,581 --> 00:02:30,458
Now the area under this graph.

55
00:02:30,458 --> 00:02:31,694
What does it give?

56
00:02:32,044 --> 00:02:36,159
Acceleration is the change
in velocity divided by time.

57
00:02:36,159 --> 00:02:40,158
So the change in velocity
is acceleration times time.

58
00:02:40,908 --> 00:02:43,411
On the graph, that is height times width.

59
00:02:43,411 --> 00:02:44,311
The area.

60
00:02:44,874 --> 00:02:46,086
The units agree.

61
00:02:46,086 --> 00:02:50,375
Metres per second squared, times
seconds, gives metres per second.

62
00:02:51,125 --> 00:02:54,753
For Aarav, one point five
times four gives plus six.

63
00:02:54,753 --> 00:02:57,379
Minus two times three gives minus six.

64
00:02:58,079 --> 00:02:59,907
Plus six, then minus six.

65
00:02:59,907 --> 00:03:02,638
He started at rest, so he ends at rest.

66
00:03:02,638 --> 00:03:03,773
The graph knew.

67
00:03:04,423 --> 00:03:05,613
Remember this.

68
00:03:05,613 --> 00:03:10,351
The area under an acceleration-time
graph is the change in velocity.

69
00:03:11,051 --> 00:03:12,286
One famous graph.

70
00:03:12,286 --> 00:03:14,023
Throw a ball straight up.

71
00:03:14,373 --> 00:03:16,112
Take upward as positive.

72
00:03:16,112 --> 00:03:20,877
Gravity pulls down the whole time, so
the acceleration is minus nine point

73
00:03:20,877 --> 00:03:21,777
eight.

74
00:03:21,904 --> 00:03:24,597
Going up, coming down, it never changes.

75
00:03:24,597 --> 00:03:27,205
The graph is one flat line, below zero.

76
00:03:27,605 --> 00:03:28,505
Your turn.

77
00:03:28,427 --> 00:03:31,543
At the very top, the ball
stops for an instant.

78
00:03:31,543 --> 00:03:33,526
What is its acceleration there?

79
00:03:33,526 --> 00:03:34,688
Pause and think.

80
00:03:37,888 --> 00:03:39,990
Still nine point eight, downward!

81
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:43,800
The velocity is zero for an
instant, but it is still changing.

82
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,874
That is why the ball comes back.

83
00:03:46,524 --> 00:03:48,260
Now, one worked example.

84
00:03:48,610 --> 00:03:51,523
A scooter moves at four metres per second.

85
00:03:51,523 --> 00:03:56,073
It accelerates at two metres per
second squared for three seconds, then

86
00:03:56,073 --> 00:03:58,245
at minus one for four seconds.

87
00:03:58,245 --> 00:03:59,985
Find its final velocity.

88
00:04:00,485 --> 00:04:01,444
First area.

89
00:04:01,444 --> 00:04:03,538
Two times three is plus six.

90
00:04:04,288 --> 00:04:05,371
Second area.

91
00:04:05,371 --> 00:04:08,071
Minus one times four is minus four.

92
00:04:08,821 --> 00:04:13,461
Total change in velocity is six minus
four, plus two metres per second.

93
00:04:14,211 --> 00:04:16,053
Add it to the starting four.

94
00:04:16,053 --> 00:04:18,738
The final velocity is
six metres per second.

95
00:04:19,388 --> 00:04:20,854
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:21,204 --> 00:04:25,930
Many students say the area under an
acceleration-time graph is the final

97
00:04:25,930 --> 00:04:26,830
velocity.

98
00:04:27,112 --> 00:04:29,509
It is only the change in velocity.

99
00:04:29,509 --> 00:04:31,734
Always add the starting velocity.

100
00:04:31,734 --> 00:04:34,647
Here, four plus two gives six, not two.

101
00:04:35,297 --> 00:04:36,731
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:36,731 --> 00:04:38,060
As graph questions.

103
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:42,720
Read the area for the change in
velocity, or turn one graph into another.

104
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:44,511
Exam level now.

105
00:04:44,511 --> 00:04:47,417
A particle starts at
two metres per second.

106
00:04:47,417 --> 00:04:51,888
Its acceleration is plus three for
two seconds, then minus two for the

107
00:04:51,888 --> 00:04:53,230
next four seconds.

108
00:04:53,230 --> 00:04:55,723
What is its velocity at six seconds?

109
00:04:55,723 --> 00:04:56,964
Pause and solve.

110
00:05:02,364 --> 00:05:03,607
The answer is B.

111
00:05:03,607 --> 00:05:04,507
Zero.

112
00:05:04,647 --> 00:05:07,486
The first area is three
times two, plus six.

113
00:05:07,486 --> 00:05:10,509
The second is minus two
times four, minus eight.

114
00:05:11,009 --> 00:05:12,659
The change is minus two.

115
00:05:12,659 --> 00:05:15,715
Add the starting two,
and the velocity is zero.

116
00:05:15,715 --> 00:05:18,968
Minus two forgets the
start, and that is the trap.

117
00:05:19,568 --> 00:05:21,029
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:21,029 --> 00:05:22,531
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:25,731 --> 00:05:28,567
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:05:28,967 --> 00:05:29,867
One.

121
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:34,016
What does a flat line above zero
on an acceleration-time graph mean?

122
00:05:34,916 --> 00:05:35,816
Two.

123
00:05:35,439 --> 00:05:39,116
What does the area under an
acceleration-time graph give?

124
00:05:40,016 --> 00:05:40,916
Three.

125
00:05:40,567 --> 00:05:44,206
What is the acceleration of a
ball at the top of its flight?

126
00:05:45,306 --> 00:05:48,012
Next time: one graph, two secret tools.

127
00:05:48,012 --> 00:05:51,919
Slope and area, and how to read
any motion graph in seconds.
